        "image_position": "left",
        "eyebrow": "01 / Tianyi Pavilion",
        "heading": "",
        "text": "<p>For 450 years, Tianyi Pavilion has stood as more than a library. Founded in the mid-16th century by Fan Qin, a Ming dynasty official who spent his career collecting books from every corner of the empire, it is the oldest surviving private library in China and one of the oldest in the world. Its name comes from the I Ching: \"Heaven creates water, earth completes it in six.\" Water conquers fire—the greatest threat to any house of books. The architecture itself was a prayer for permanence: a pool before the pavilion, a fire lane separating the library from the family's living quarters, windows placed for ventilation, herbs tucked between pages to ward off insects.</p>",
