      "type": "thyling-story",
      "settings": {
        "image_fill": true,
        "image_position": "left",
        "eyebrow": "",
        "heading": "",
        "text": "<p>Within those collections, Chinese medicine held a quiet but enduring place. Tianyi Pavilion's holdings include hundreds of medical texts, among them rare manuscripts and Ming-era editions that survive nowhere else—treatises on acupuncture, formularies, works of diagnosis and pharmacology that trace the long conversation of Chinese healing across dynasties. The pavilion did not merely store these texts. It kept them alive long enough for later generations to read them. We carry that inheritance forward—not as something to remain in old books, but as rituals to be lived every day. Drawing on this legacy and the medical tradition of the Fan family, we treat old texts not as relics of the past but as living knowledge. Their intricate herbal combinations and carefully considered order of use are reimagined as simple, approachable products made for modern everyday life.</p><p></p>",
